  "id": "Compound::DB01197 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Captopril may be associated with Coronary Artery Disease through a series of interconnected pathways. Captopril, an antihypertensive agent, is linked to an increase in serum potassium levels, which is mediated by Valsartan, Eplerenone, or Ramipril. These medications, in turn, are associated with the treatment of Coronary Artery Disease. The shared mechanism of action involves the regulation of blood pressure and the modulation of electrolyte balance, which may influence cardiovascular health. The evidence highlights the role of antihypertensive agents in the pathophysiology of Coronary Artery Disease, with Captopril potentially contributing through its effects on potassium levels and its classification as an amide. The integration of these pathways underscores the complex interplay between drug effects, physiological responses, and disease outcomes.",
